{
  "_doc": [
    "RECT CORNER PROMOTION -- what a Partial control-point move does to a Rect, in",
    "every implementation: the emitted Polygon's points, and the control-point",
    "selection the next drag sample must address.",
    "",
    "RATIFIED ANSWER (3) (JYH, 2026-07-27; transcripts/EDIT_SEMANTICS_FREEZE.md",
    "section 8): rx/ry have no counterpart on Polygon, so a corner drag FLATTENS the",
    "rounding into the emitted points (WYSIWYG at promotion) instead of letting it",
    "evaporate. Each corner becomes one run of FLATTEN_STEPS + 1 points sampled from",
    "that corner's quadratic, in control-point order (0 top-left, 1 top-right,",
    "2 bottom-right, 3 bottom-left); a dragged corner translates its whole run. A",
    "SQUARE rect gives four one-point runs, i.e. the four corners.",
    "",
    "Radii clamp as the renderer's rounded-rect path does: if both are <= 0 the",
    "rect is square; otherwise rx = clamp(rx, 0, w/2) and ry = clamp(ry, 0, h/2).",
    "So one positive radius beside a zero one still yields arc runs, which the",
    "`rx_only` and `negative_rx_positive_ry` vectors pin.",
    "",
    "`remapped_cps` is the selection after the move. A corner drag is a",
    "multi-sample gesture fed an incremental delta per mousemove, so once corner i",
    "has become a run of indices the selection must name the whole run, or the",
    "second sample drags a single arc point and shreds the corner.",
    "",
    "A Partial selection naming all four corners is `is_all` in every",
    "implementation, so that vector stays a Rect and translates, rounding intact.",
    "",
    "Generated by jas_dioxus (`regenerate_rect_corner_promotion`, ignored by",
    "default). Every implementation computes the same quadratic in the same",
    "operation order, and each consumer compares points within 1e-9."
  ],
